{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgajCXJKTpo8zePsauFoK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I replaced the `sorry` with a proof of `revAcc_nil`. `lean Main.lean` now runs with no errors or warnings.\n\nInducting on `xs` directly doesn't work, because the accumulator stops being `[]` after the first step. So I added a more general helper lemma first:\n\n```lean\ntheorem revAcc_eq (xs acc : List α) : revAcc xs acc = myRev xs ++ acc := by\n  induction xs generalizing acc with\n  | nil => simp [revAcc, myRev]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [revAcc, myRev, ih]\n\ntheorem revAcc_nil (xs : List α) : revAcc xs [] = myRev xs := by\n  simp [revAcc_eq]\n```\n\n`revAcc_eq` says that running `revAcc` with any accumulator gives `myRev xs ++ acc`. Using `generalizing acc` lets the induction step use that fact with the new accumulator `x :: acc`. `revAcc_nil` is then the case `acc = []`, where `simp` drops the `++ []`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":644,"cache_read_input_tokens":7960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":644},"output_tokens":359,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f162779e-2686-48a4-80f7-27fc76073a53","uuid":"2b7e40f7-55fc-4566-9a4e-558fcc49bed2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T17:42:55.258Z","request_id":"req_011CgajCX5RcVwkGtjhBZMf8"}
